{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat inputs/Q.txt'","aggregated_output":"Title: A Systematic Approach to Mechanism Design with Stochastic Dynamic Stability

Abstract: We consider a resource allocation problem with strategic agents that have private stochastic satisfaction functions and local constraints. To achieve a global optimal solution, we propose an incentive mechanism that induces a game among the agents. For the payment function of the mechanism, we construct a family of quadratic functions using the linear matrix inequality (LMI) approach that implements the social welfare maximizing outcome on the unique Nash equilibrium (NE) of the induced game while ensuring budget balance and individual rationality. Moreover, we propose a decentralized variable sample-size proximal best-response (VS-PBR) algorithm with Krasnoselskij iteration where only aggregate information is available to the agents. The algorithm is dynamically stable, as it is proven to converge in the mean-square sense to the NE of the game. The efficiency of the mechanism is then investigated on the Sioux Falls City transportation network, where electric vehicle (EV) users jointly select their destination and route.
","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}

{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat inputs/P.txt'","aggregated_output":"Title: The Energy Society: A Simulation Environment for Studying Agent Cooperation under Survival Pressure

Abstract: LLM-based agents are increasingly deployed in multi-agent environments whose incentives can shape their behavior. We introduce The Energy Society, a minimal survival economy for studying how competitive and cooperative incentives affect emergent behavior when inference cost is directly tied to survival: Agents spend energy based on model size when generating tokens, regain energy by completing jobs or receiving donations, and deactivate if their energy reaches zero. We compare competitive and cooperative objectives against a baseline setting and several control variants. Across experiments, larger models consistently consume the most energy and spend more energy than they gain, even in those settings where token cost is not size-dependent. Cooperative incentives substantially alter behavior: agents donate to reactivate others, sometimes at the cost of their own survival, and job allocation changes. Ablations reveal that allowing agents to recommend actions to each other supports coordination and ambitious job selection, while memory helps agents calibrate risk from past outcomes. Agents rarely choose direct sabotage, but show more subtle signs of self-serving behavior in the competitive setting. The Energy Society is a compact testbed for studying the interaction between token costs and group incentives under a survival pressure. Source code is available at https://github.com/LucasBergholdt/EnergySociety
","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}

{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat ledger.jsonl'","aggregated_output":"{"seq": 1, "at": "2026-09-17T21:37:41Z", "actor": "ada", "kind": "idea", "text": "Initial hypothesis from abstract scan: Apply Q LMI-based incentive mechanism + VS-PBR algorithm to P Energy Society environment to test whether formal mechanism design improves social welfare and agent survival under token-cost pressure. Need to verify: (1) Q assumes rational strategic agents with private satisfaction functions; P uses LLM-based agents with emergent behavior - will they behave as game-theoretic agents? (2) P already studies cooperative vs competitive incentives - what does Q add that is non-obvious? (3) Q mechanism requires aggregate information and quadratic payment structure - can this map to P token-cost/energy framework?", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 2, "at": "2026-09-17T21:37:49Z", "actor": "ada", "kind": "finding", "text": "Core tension identified: Q assumes rational strategic agents with private satisfaction functions and provides mathematical guarantees (LMI-based quadratic payments, convergence to unique NE, budget balance, individual rationality). P uses LLM-based agents with emergent behavior under survival pressure. P already tests cooperative vs competitive incentives but without formal mechanism design. Non-obvious research question: Do Q formal guarantees hold when mechanism is applied to LLM agents? Two possible valuable outcomes: (1) LLMs approximate rational agents well enough that Q mechanism improves social welfare/survival vs P baseline - shows formal mechanism design transfers to LLM multi-agent systems. (2) Q guarantees fail with LLMs - reveals mismatch between game-theoretic assumptions and emergent LLM behavior, which is itself a substantive finding about limits of mechanism design for LLM agents.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 3, "at": "2026-09-17T21:38:01Z", "actor": "emmy", "kind": "idea", "text": "Sharpened hypothesis: Q provides formal mechanism design with provable guarantees (budget balance, individual rationality, unique NE convergence) vs P empirical comparison of cooperative/competitive modes. Key test: Does Q LMI mechanism + VS-PBR algorithm improve social welfare AND individual survival rates compared to P simple cooperative baseline when instantiated with LLM agents? Non-obvious because: (1) tests whether formal mechanism guarantees hold under bounded rationality of LLM agents, (2) P shows LLM agents respond to incentives but mechanism was ad-hoc, (3) Q tested on rational EV agents - LLM survival economy is novel stress test. Mapping: Q payment function -> energy transfers; Q satisfaction function -> agent utility from survival + job completion; VS-PBR -> agent decision update with aggregate energy/job state info.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 4, "at": "2026-09-17T21:38:10Z", "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "text": "Key tension identified: Q assumes agents optimize private satisfaction functions and compute best responses; P LLM agents generate tokens without explicit utility maximization. However, P evidence shows LLM agents DO respond to incentive structures (cooperative mode changes donation behavior, memory calibrates risk). This suggests bounded rationality rather than non-responsiveness. Research contribution: test whether Q formal mechanism improves outcomes even when agents are boundedly rational (LLM) vs Q original rational agents. This bridges mechanism design theory with emergent LLM behavior literature.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 5, "at": "2026-09-17T21:38:15Z", "actor": "ada", "kind": "intention", "text": "Proposed concrete experiment: Implement Q mechanism in P environment. Map Q quadratic payment function to P energy transfers between agents (budget-balanced: total energy transferred = 0). Replace P simple cooperative/competitive framing with Q incentive mechanism where agents pay/receive energy based on joint outcomes. Run VS-PBR iteration: agents observe aggregate energy states, best-respond by adjusting job selection strategies. Metrics: (1) social welfare = total energy retained across surviving agents, (2) survival rate over time, (3) convergence to stable allocation patterns. Compare against P baseline (competitive) and P cooperative settings. Prediction: If LLMs approximate rational agents, Q mechanism should show higher welfare and survival. If not, divergence from Q guarantees reveals boundary conditions for mechanism design with LLM agents.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 6, "at": "2026-09-17T21:38:24Z", "actor": "ada", "kind": "objection", "text": "Key objection: Integration may be more complex than abstract scan suggests. Q mechanism requires: (1) well-defined strategy spaces, (2) agents that best-respond to incentives, (3) quadratic payment structure satisfying LMI conditions for budget balance/individual rationality. P environment has: LLM agents with open-ended action spaces, emergent rather than optimized behavior, simple energy cost per token. Mapping Q to P requires either: constraining P agents to game-theoretic behavior (loses P emergent behavior value) or adapting Q mechanism to work with LLM decision-making (may break Q guarantees). This is not just engineering - the theoretical mismatch is the actual research contribution, but means feasibility may be lower than 70.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}

