{"seq": 5, "at": "2026-09-17T21:42:18Z", "actor": "ada", "kind": "finding", "text": "Sharpened analysis:\n\nQ peer scoring requires: (1) agents have private information, (2) scoring rules create incentives for truthful revelation, (3) higher-order beliefs can discipline.\n\nP AR structure: reviewer observes code, critic observes (code, review), main agent observes (code, review, critique).\n\nCRITICAL INSIGHT: Critic observes reviewer output but NOT reviewer private reasoning. If reviewer has private information (e.g., uncertainty, alternative interpretations not reported), critic cannot fully discipline. This matches Q one-sided imitation: capabilities/reasoning can be concealed.\n\nCONCRETE PREDICTION from Q:\nAR false-consensus occurs when reviewer and critic share common prior but reviewer conceals uncertainty (sandbagging). Explicit disagreement prompting works because it forces revelation, not because it adds disagreement per se.\n\nTESTABLE: Vary whether reviewer must report confidence/alternatives. Q predicts: mandatory uncertainty revelation reduces false-consensus more than disagreement prompts alone.\n\nThis is non-obvious: P attributed false-consensus to insufficient evidence; Q suggests it may be concealed uncertainty + lack of revelation mechanism.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
